          %h1#section_table_of_contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#overview"}  Overview
              %li
                %a{:href => "#notions_of_sets_and_elements"} Notions of sets and elements
              %li
                %a{:href => "#see_also"}  See also
          %h2#overview  Overview
          %p
            Simply sorted set theory is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            which is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+theory"} first order theory
            without
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sorts"} sorts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+order+logic"} first order logic
            and whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domains+of+discourse"} domains of discourse
            (called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sorts"} sorts
            here) do not on other domains of discourse. In addition, membership
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in A
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation"} relation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/judgment"} judgment
            as in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependently+sorted+set+theory"} dependently sorted set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theories"} material set theories
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theories"} structural set theories
          %p Simply sorted set theory could be distinguished between how many sorts the theory has; these include
          %ul
            %li
              one-sorted set theory, which include certain presentations of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mostowski+set+theory"} Mostowski set theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fully+formal+ETCS"} fully formal ETCS
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFA"} ZFA
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-sorted+set+theory"}> two-sorted set theory
              , which include certain presentations of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFA"} ZFA
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/three-sorted+set+theory"}> three-sorted set theory
              , which include
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/three-sorted+SEAR"} three-sorted SEAR
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/three-sorted+structural+ZFC"} three-sorted structural ZFC
              as well as certain presentations of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS+with+elements"} ETCS with elements
            %li
              four-sorted set theory, which include certain presentations of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEPS"} SEPS
          %h2#notions_of_sets_and_elements Notions of sets and elements
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p A notion of set and element in a simply sorted first-order theory consists of:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  A sort
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  of probable sets. By probable set, we mean that certain terms of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  are to be considered as the sets in the theory.
              %li
                %p
                  A sort
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  of probable elements. By probable element, we mean that certain terms of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  are to be considered as the elements in the theory.
              %li
                %p
                  A binary predicate in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi S
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msub
                            %mo =
                            %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)=_S(-)
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/definitional+equality"} definitional equality
                    of sets
              %li
                %p
                  A binary predicate in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi E
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msub
                            %mo =
                            %mi E
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)=_E(-)
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
                    of elements
              %li
                %p
                  A unary predicate in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi S
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} set
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{set}(-)
                  %em being a set
              %li
                %p
                  A unary predicate in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi E
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} element
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{element}(-)
                  %em being an element
              %li
                %p
                  A binary predicate in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  on the left and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
                  on the right,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ∈
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)\in(-)
                  %em membership
            %p such that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %msub
                          %mo =
                          %mi E
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)=_E(-)
                  is an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
              %li
                %p
                  if
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi a
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi b
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \in b
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                  is an element and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                  is a set
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo .
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} element
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall a:E.\forall b:S.(a \in b) \to \mathrm{element}(a) \wedge \mathrm{set}(b)
            %ul
              %li
                weak extensionality for sets and elements: for all
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo :
                      %mi S
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a:S
                and for all
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo :
                        %mi S
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b:S
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %msub
                        %mo =
                        %mi S
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a =_S b
                if and only if, for all
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi c
                        %mo :
                        %mi E
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c:E
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                is an element, then
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in a
                if and only if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \in b
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi a
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo .
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi b
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∧
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %msub
                      %mo =
                      %mi S
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mi E
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} element
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⇔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall a:S.\forall b:S.(\mathrm{set}(a) \wedge \mathrm{set}(b)) \implies ((a =_S b) \iff \forall c:E.(\mathrm{element}(c) \implies ((c \in a) \iff (c \in b))))
            %p
          %p
            In one-sorted set theory, the sorts
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi E
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            are the same sort.
